WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.343
- Welcome back, y'all.

00:00:01.343 --> 00:00:02.370
I'm hanging out with Elle King.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.461
Once again, give it up.

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:05.700
(audience cheering)

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.320
So May is Asian-American

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.180
and Pacific Islander Heritage Month,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.770
which celebrates the rich history

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.800
and cultural heritage for
tens of millions of Americans.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.650
A group of authors are working
to get more children's books

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.860
with AAPI stories and characters
into school libraries,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:20.790
'cause that's important.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.900
And here to talk about it,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.130
please welcome authors

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.050
Yobe Qiu and Serena Li.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.217
Everybody give it up.

00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:27.050
(audience cheering)

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:27.883
So, meet Elle.

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:29.103
Everybody meet everybody.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.700
So, Yobe, this idea came
from a very unexpected place.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.470
Tell everybody when y'all thought of it.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.450
- I was watching Oscars that one night

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.950
like all of America was

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.530
and I was completely overjoyed by the wind

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.390
of "Everything Everywhere All at Once".

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.560
The excellent--
(audience clapping)

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.087
Yeah, yeah.

00:00:45.087 --> 00:00:46.650
But the light bulb went off

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.487
for me when Michelle Yeoh said,

00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:51.900
"I want all the little
girls that look like me

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.290
to know what's possible and dream big".

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.270
And then I thought I have to do something

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.860
to extend that representation.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.810
I called up my friend Serena

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.640
and our two other author friends

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.900
and I was like, do you wanna join me

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.730
on this campaign where we
fundraise to get hundreds of books

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.530
and send them to school districts,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.510
five different states in America.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.540
And we hopped on a Zoom call
and the rest was history.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.280
But we got so excited

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.113
that we named--
(audience clapping)

00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:18.946
Thank you.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.480
But we got so excited,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.377
we actually named the campaign

00:01:23.377 --> 00:01:26.160
"Asian American Books
Everywhere All at Once".

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.930
- Oh, I love that.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.120
And 'cause that's where the idea spring.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:30.953
That's amazing.

00:01:30.953 --> 00:01:31.950
So, to both of you,

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.420
you say, no matter where you're from,

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.590
no one has the same story.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.970
So explain what that means to you.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:36.840
- Yeah, exactly.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.707
Well even though Yobe

00:01:37.707 --> 00:01:40.590
and I both identify as Asian Americans,

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.710
but our story couldn't
be any more different.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.140
I, myself, I was born in China

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.620
and moved to the States at age of 14.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.050
Without speaking a nick of English.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.340
Right and--

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.270
- That's hard.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.410
- It was really hard.
- At fourteen as well.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.704
Fourteen's already a hard age, right?

00:01:57.704 --> 00:01:58.537
Yeah.

00:01:58.537 --> 00:02:00.330
- Yeah. In fact I learned
English through songs.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.610
And Kelly, your song, "Miss Independent".

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.443
- Oh.

00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:05.134
- Held a whole other level of meaning.

00:02:05.134 --> 00:02:06.814
(audience cheering)

00:02:06.814 --> 00:02:07.647
So, so yeah.

00:02:07.647 --> 00:02:09.690
As a first generation immigrant,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.970
living in a place without much diversity,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.110
I actually have neighbors

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.587
and classmates that would ask,

00:02:14.587 --> 00:02:17.247
"Hey do Asians actually speak Asian?"

00:02:20.065 --> 00:02:20.898
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:02:20.898 --> 00:02:22.470
- So now that I have a daughter of my own

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.740
and I always long to pass on my heritage

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:26.970
and language to her,

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.280
and that's how I started
my publishing business

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.840
called "Duck Duck Books".

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.470
And I made multilingual kids
books with my mother tongue,

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:35.730
like Cantonese and Mandarin.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.260
- [Kelly] Yeah, beautiful.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.660
- Yeah. We have very similar experiences

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.640
but also very different experiences.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.100
I was so lucky, I grew up in New York City

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.990
in the middle of--
- Very diverse.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.540
- Very diverse, lower
East side and Chinatown

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.260
and where diversity was absolutely
embraced and celebrated.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.270
And then I went to become a teacher

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.630
and I was trying to get books
that featured Asian children

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.740
and families and cultures,
but there just weren't many.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.680
And I decided the best
time to write a book

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.530
was to write something
for Lunar New Year time,

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.090
because I was teaching
a lesson at that time.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.450
I was creating my own
material, like all teachers do.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.780
And then I went on to research a ton

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.930
of how different Asian
communities celebrate

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:19.830
our Lunar New Year.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.420
And I learned so much, Kelly.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.000
I've learned about how the Thai community,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.460
how the Vietnamese community,
how the Indian community,

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.020
Chinese and Korean community
celebrate this holiday.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.760
And throughout this whole process,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.170
what I was most proud of is,

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.610
we are living in a great time,

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.030
where representation is
really exciting for us now,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.100
but I just feel like there just isn't

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.590
that enough books for children.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.330
- Absolutely.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.250
My kids are fortunate
cause I didn't grow up,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.780
and I don't know how you grew up,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.710
but I didn't grow up around a lot

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.890
of diversity in half of my childhood,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.360
and it was one of those things,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.470
that I look at my kids

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.150
and they celebrate all
these different holidays

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.460
that I'm learning about as an adult.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.293
- Right.

00:04:00.293 --> 00:04:01.126
- Like, you know, 'cause
I never was taught

00:04:01.126 --> 00:04:01.959
when I was a kid.
- Right.

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:04.710
- But they're so exposed
because they live in a city

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.390
that kind of is like how you grew up.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.440
It's so diverse.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.850
But like I didn't grow up with that.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.100
Like, knowing a lot
about different cultures

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.830
and their, you know, their
holidays or anything like that.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.150
Did you grow up like that?

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.110
- Halfway.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.630
Ohio? Not so much.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.400
My grandmother's from Manila

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.210
and when she moved to
America she became a teacher

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:28.680
and so I had a really big
extended Filipino family

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.900
in San Francisco, that
was a really massive part

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.733
of my upbringing.

00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:33.960
So, this is really
beautiful because I don't,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.820
since my grandmother's passed on,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.210
I don't know what ways to show my son part

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.220
of his culture and heritage.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.510
And so this is really beautiful

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.448
that you guys are making these.

00:04:43.448 --> 00:04:44.281
- [Yobe] Thank you.

00:04:44.281 --> 00:04:45.270
- It's really important. That's awesome.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.103
- Thank you.

00:04:46.103 --> 00:04:47.907
- Yeah, it's super important.
(audience clapping)

00:04:47.907 --> 00:04:50.760
Well, Serena and Yobe,
since it's AAPI month,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.400
what are some great books that
you recommend for parents?

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.233
- Yes.

00:04:54.233 --> 00:04:56.550
So I absolutely adore one

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.430
of the authors called Tracy Guan.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.950
She's actually right here in the audience.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:03.240
Hi, Tracy.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.801
- Oh, what's up?
- Yes.

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:05.634
(audience clapping)

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:08.040
So, Tracy is actually one of us four

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.810
that are doing this campaign together

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.800
and she has a book called
"Lunchtime with Samnang",

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.950
which talks about this
Cambodian boy, you know,

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:18.690
and his adventure during lunchtime,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.570
where he learns more about his heritage

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:25.018
as he was tasting these yummy,
you know, Cambodian dishes.

00:05:25.018 --> 00:05:25.851
- Yeah.

00:05:25.851 --> 00:05:28.230
- And if you have older children,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.640
middle school or high school,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.590
I recommend a book by Vincent Yee.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:32.850
That's another one of our authors.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.260
Say Hi, Vincent.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.759
- What's up, Vincent?
(audience clapping)

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:37.920
- What's up, Vincent?

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.150
I really like that series
because it shows Asian Americans

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.310
that they can be superheroes

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.770
and I think everyone
should have the opportunity

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.510
to see themselves as superheroes

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.120
in stories that they read
and TVs that they watch.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.260
- Yeah.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.340
Especially kids growing up.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.260
That's so important.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.050
Well, Scholastic shares
your mission of making sure

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.750
that all children are being
able to see themselves

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.430
and their stories reflected in the books

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:03.300
that they're reading,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.440
which is why they're excited

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.330
to donate a thousand books written

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.870
by AAPI authors and
featuring AAPI characters.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.540
- So excited.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.280
- Because, like I said, it's important

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.780
for everyone to see themselves,

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.630
but it's also important for
every kid to see other kids

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:19.740
that aren't like them. You know?

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:20.573
- Absolutely.
- Right.

00:06:20.573 --> 00:06:22.130
That's just as important. I think.

